.TH SDL_CreateThreadWithProperties 3 "SDL 3.5.0" "Simple Directmedia Layer" "SDL3 FUNCTIONS"
.SH NAME
SDL_CreateThreadWithProperties \- Create a new thread with with the specified properties.
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B #include <SDL3/SDL_thread.h>
.PP
.BI "SDL_Thread * SDL_CreateThreadWithProperties(SDL_PropertiesID props);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
These are the supported properties:
.PP

\(bu
.BR SDL_PROP_THREAD_CREATE_ENTRY_FUNCTION_POINTER :
an
.BR SDL_ThreadFunction
value that will be called at the start of the new thread\(aqs life\[char46] Required\[char46]
.PP
\(bu
.BR SDL_PROP_THREAD_CREATE_NAME_STRING :
the name of the new thread, which might be available to debuggers\[char46] Optional, defaults to NULL\[char46]
.PP
\(bu
.BR SDL_PROP_THREAD_CREATE_USERDATA_POINTER :
an arbitrary app-defined pointer, which is passed to the entry function on the new thread, as its only parameter\[char46] Optional, defaults to NULL\[char46]
.PP
\(bu
.BR SDL_PROP_THREAD_CREATE_STACKSIZE_NUMBER :
the size, in bytes, of the new thread\(aqs stack\[char46] Optional, defaults to 0 (system-defined default)\[char46]
.PP
SDL makes an attempt to report
.BR SDL_PROP_THREAD_CREATE_NAME_STRING
to the system, so that debuggers can display it\[char46] Not all platforms support this\[char46]
.PP
Thread naming is a little complicated: Most systems have very small limits for the string length (Haiku has 32 bytes, Linux currently has 16, Visual C++ 6\[char46]0 has _nine_!), and possibly other arbitrary rules\[char46] You\(aqll have to see what happens with your system\(aqs debugger\[char46] The name should be UTF-8 (but using the naming limits of C identifiers is a better bet)\[char46] There are no requirements for thread naming conventions, so long as the string is null-terminated UTF-8, but these guidelines are helpful in choosing a name:
.PP
https://stackoverflow\[char46]com/questions/149932/naming-conventions-for-threads
.PP
If a system imposes requirements, SDL will try to munge the string for it (truncate, etc), but the original string contents will be available from
.BR SDL_GetThreadName ()\[char46]
.PP
The size (in bytes) of the new stack can be specified with
.BR SDL_PROP_THREAD_CREATE_STACKSIZE_NUMBER \[char46]
Zero means \(dquse the system default\(dq which might be wildly different between platforms\[char46] x86 Linux generally defaults to eight megabytes, an embedded device might be a few kilobytes instead\[char46] You generally need to specify a stack that is a multiple of the system\(aqs page size (in many cases, this is 4 kilobytes, but check your system documentation)\[char46]
.PP
Note that this \(dqfunction\(dq is actually a macro that calls an internal function with two extra parameters not listed here; they are hidden through preprocessor macros and are needed to support various C runtimes at the point of the function call\[char46] Language bindings that aren\(aqt using the C headers will need to deal with this\[char46]
.PP
The actual symbol in SDL is
.BR SDL_CreateThreadWithPropertiesRuntime ,
so there is no symbol clash, but trying to load an SDL shared library and look for
.BR "" \(dq SDL_CreateThreadWithProperties \(dq
will fail\[char46]
.PP
Usually, apps should just call this function the same way on every platform and let the macros hide the details\[char46]

.SH FUNCTION PARAMETERS
.TP
.I props
the properties to use\[char46]
.SH RETURN VALUE
Returns an opaque pointer to the new thread object on success, NULL if the new thread could not be created; call
.BR SDL_GetError ()
for more information\[char46]

.SH THREAD SAFETY
It is safe to call this function from any thread\[char46]

.SH AVAILABILITY
This function is available since SDL 3\[char46]2\[char46]0\[char46]

.SH SEE ALSO
.BR SDL_CreateThread (3),
.BR SDL_WaitThread (3)
